  "title": "Erdős Problem #254",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Let $A\\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$ be such that $ \\lvert A\\cap [1,2x]\\rvert -\\lvert A\\cap [1,x]\\rvert \\to \\infty\\textrm{ as }x\\to \\infty $ and $ \\sum_{n\\in A} \\{ \\theta n\\}=\\infty $ for every $\\theta\\in (0,1)$, where $\\{x\\}$ is the distance of $x$ from the nearest integer. Then every sufficiently large integer is the sum of distinct elements of $A$.",
